## Behaviour and content

State the purpose of each optional category in plain language. Accept and reject must have equivalent prominence. Provide a durable route back to preferences, and honour a rejection before loading optional code. In a real implementation, confirmation follows a successful save; failures leave the choice available with an explanation.

Keep the banner in document flow so it does not cover focused content. Do not use a preselected checkbox or a dismiss action as implied consent. Document storage lifetime and how the preference works across browsers using verified service facts.

## Accessibility and testing

Check logical reading order, meaningful control labels and keyboard reachability. Test reject, accept, revisiting, changing a preference, unavailable storage and JavaScript failure. Inspect actual network and storage behaviour; the visual example alone cannot verify it.

## Home Assistant boundary and source

The theme does not manage authentication, telemetry or cookie consent. Preserve native Home Assistant preferences. Consult the [GOV.UK Cookie banner guidance](https://design-system.service.gov.uk/components/cookie-banner/) when building a separate service, and review that service’s privacy requirements before connecting this reference.
